      title: "The Corrupt Bargain and the Election of 1828",
      body: `## Four candidates

By 1824 the Federalist Party was dead, and the Republicans had no effective way to choose a single candidate. The congressional caucus nominated William H. Crawford of Georgia, the secretary of the treasury, but by now the caucus was widely denounced as undemocratic, and Crawford had suffered a paralyzing stroke. Three other Republicans ran: **John Quincy Adams** of Massachusetts, Monroe's secretary of state; **Henry Clay** of Kentucky, the Speaker of the House and champion of the American System; and **Andrew Jackson** of Tennessee, the hero of New Orleans, nominated by the Tennessee legislature. (Calhoun, who had also hoped to run, settled for the vice presidency, which he won easily.)

Jackson won the most popular votes, about 41 percent, and the most electoral votes, 99. Adams won 84, Crawford 41, and Clay 37. Since no one had a majority, the Twelfth Amendment sent the election to the House of Representatives, which had to choose among the top three. Clay, who had finished fourth, was eliminated, but as Speaker he had great influence in the House.

## The bargain

Clay disliked Jackson, whom he regarded as a mere "military chieftain," and he shared many of Adams's views on national economic development. He threw his support to Adams, who was elected by the House in February 1825 on the first ballot. A few days later, Adams appointed Clay secretary of state, the office that had been the stepping-stone to the presidency for Jefferson, Madison, Monroe, and Adams himself.

Jackson's supporters cried that a "**corrupt bargain**" had cheated the people's choice. There is no evidence of an explicit deal, but the appearance was damning, and Jackson never forgave either man. Jackson resigned from the Senate and began campaigning for 1828 almost immediately.

## Adams's presidency

John Quincy Adams was one of the most intelligent and experienced men ever to hold the presidency, and one of the least successful. His first annual message in 1825 proposed an ambitious program of national development: roads and canals, a national university, a naval academy, a national observatory—"lighthouses of the skies," he called them, a phrase that was widely mocked—and federal support for science and exploration. He urged Congress not to be "palsied by the will of our constituents," a phrase his opponents used to portray him as an aristocrat contemptuous of the people. Congress, increasingly controlled by Jackson's supporters, rejected most of his program.

Adams also refused to play the new politics. He would not use patronage to build a party, retaining even officials who opposed him. He insisted on honoring a treaty with the Creeks that Georgia's governor resisted. His presidency was a frustrated one, and he would later find his true vocation as a congressman from Massachusetts, where from 1831 until his death in 1848 he was the House's most eloquent opponent of slavery's power.

## The Tariff of Abominations

In 1828 Jackson's supporters in Congress, seeking to win votes in the Middle States and the West, crafted a tariff with very high duties on imported raw materials as well as manufactured goods. Some apparently hoped that New England would vote against it, so that Adams's region would be blamed for its defeat. Instead, it passed. The **Tariff of Abominations**, as its Southern opponents called it, raised duties to their highest level before the Civil War. It enraged the South, which exported cotton and imported manufactured goods, and it would lead directly to the nullification crisis.

## The campaign of 1828

The election of 1828 was a rematch between Jackson and Adams, and one of the dirtiest in American history. Jackson's supporters, now calling themselves **Democrats**, organized committees, newspapers, and rallies across the country; hickory poles, in honor of "Old Hickory," went up in town squares. They portrayed Adams as an aristocrat who had bought the presidency and had used public money to buy a billiard table for the White House (he had paid for it himself). They even accused him, falsely, of procuring a young American woman for the Russian tsar while serving as minister in St. Petersburg.

Adams's supporters responded in kind. They denounced Jackson as a murderer, citing his duels and his execution of militiamen during the Creek War, and distributed the "Coffin Handbill," decorated with coffins representing the six militiamen he had ordered shot for desertion in 1815. Most painfully, they charged that Jackson's wife, **Rachel**, was a bigamist and an adulteress: she had married Jackson in 1791 before her divorce from her first husband was final, a technicality they had corrected with a second ceremony in 1794. A pro-Adams editor asked whether "a convicted adulteress and her paramour husband" should be placed "in the highest offices of this free and Christian land."

## The people's president

Jackson won decisively, with 56 percent of the popular vote and 178 electoral votes to Adams's 83. He swept the South and West and carried Pennsylvania and much of New York. A few weeks later, in December 1828, Rachel Jackson died of a heart attack at their home, the Hermitage. Jackson, grief-stricken, blamed his enemies for her death and never forgave them.

His inauguration on March 4, 1829, became legendary. Thousands of ordinary people came to Washington to see their hero sworn in, and after the ceremony a crowd followed him to the White House reception, where they broke china, stood on furniture in muddy boots, and pressed so close that Jackson had to escape through a window. The crowd was lured out only when servants carried tubs of punch onto the lawn. To Jackson's supporters, it was the people taking possession of their government. To his critics, such as Justice Joseph Story, who watched with dismay, it was "the reign of King Mob."

> **Pause and consider.** Was the "corrupt bargain" corrupt? How might Clay have defended his choice?`,
